/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · Space & Cosmos (batch z) */
WB.L("eclipses", "space", "beginner", "Eclipses: the cosmic coincidence",
  "For a few minutes, day turns to night, the temperature drops and stars appear at noon. It happens because of one of the luckiest coincidences in the solar system.",
  [
    ["A perfect fit", "The Sun is about 400 times wider than the Moon but also about 400 times farther away, so they look almost the same size.",
      "Hold your thumb at arm's length and it can hide a whole building. The Moon does the same trick with the Sun. The Sun is roughly 400 times the Moon's diameter, yet it also sits roughly 400 times farther from us. Both discs therefore look almost exactly the same size in our sky, about half a degree across.\n\nNo other planet has a moon that fits its sun so neatly. When the Moon passes directly between Earth and the Sun, it can cover the blinding disc completely and leave the faint outer atmosphere, the corona, glowing like a silver crown. It is an accident of geometry, and we happen to live in the right era to enjoy it."],
    ["Total, annular, partial", "Because the Moon's orbit is slightly oval, some solar eclipses are total, some leave a ring of fire, and most people see only a partial one.",
      "The Moon's orbit is not a perfect circle. If an eclipse happens when the Moon is near its closest point, it looks big enough to cover the Sun entirely, giving a total eclipse. Near its farthest point, the Moon looks slightly too small and a bright ring of sunlight surrounds it. That is an annular eclipse, from the Latin annulus, meaning ring.\n\nAnyone outside the narrow central path sees only a partial eclipse, with a bite taken out of the Sun. The path of totality is usually only about 100 to 200 kilometres wide and races across Earth's surface faster than a jet airliner. That is why eclipse chasers travel the world to stand in exactly the right strip of land."],
    ["Why not every month?", "The Moon's orbit is tilted about five degrees, so eclipses only happen when the Sun, Moon and Earth line up at the crossing points.",
      "The Moon circles Earth roughly once a month, so you might expect an eclipse at every new moon. The catch is that its orbit is tilted about five degrees compared with Earth's path around the Sun. Most months the new moon slides just above or below the Sun in our sky and nothing happens.\n\nEclipses can only occur when a new or full moon lines up with one of the two points where the orbits cross. These alignments arrive in eclipse seasons, roughly every six months. Ancient Babylonian astronomers noticed that eclipses repeat in a cycle of about 18 years and 11 days, now called the saros. It let them predict eclipses long before anyone understood orbits."],
    ["The blood moon", "In a total lunar eclipse the Moon turns red because Earth's atmosphere bends sunset-coloured light onto it.",
      "In a lunar eclipse, Earth passes between the Sun and the full Moon, and our planet's shadow falls across the lunar surface. You might expect the Moon to vanish, but it usually glows deep copper or blood red. The reason is our atmosphere. Sunlight skimming past the edge of Earth is bent inwards towards the Moon, and most of its blue light is scattered out on the way, the same process that makes sunsets red. In effect, the Moon is lit by every sunrise and sunset on Earth at once.\n\nUnlike solar eclipses, lunar eclipses can be seen by anyone on the night side of the planet, and they are completely safe to watch with the naked eye."],
    ["Watching safely", "Only totality is safe to view directly; at every other stage of a solar eclipse you need certified eclipse glasses or an indirect method.",
      "Even when most of the Sun is covered, the remaining sliver is intense enough to damage the retina. The retina has no pain receptors, so you may not notice until afterwards. Ordinary sunglasses are nowhere near dark enough.\n\nSafe options include certified eclipse glasses or solar viewers meeting the ISO 12312-2 standard, or an indirect method such as a pinhole projector, where you look at an image of the Sun cast onto paper. Never look through binoculars, a camera or a telescope without a proper solar filter fitted to the front. The one exception is the brief period of totality, when the Sun is completely hidden. The moment the first bead of sunlight reappears, the glasses go back on.\n\nAustralia's turn comes on 22 July 2028, when totality crosses the country, including Sydney."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why do the Sun and Moon look almost the same size from Earth?", ["Earth's atmosphere magnifies the Moon's disc","The Sun is 400 times larger but 400 times farther away","The Moon sits closer to the Sun than Earth does","They really are almost exactly the same size"], 1, "The Sun's far greater size is cancelled out by its far greater distance."],
    [1, "What causes an annular eclipse, with a ring of sunlight around the Moon?", ["Clouds covering part of the Sun during the eclipse","Earth's shadow falling partly on the Moon","The Moon being near the far point of its orbit","The Moon being much closer to Earth than usual"], 2, "The Moon's oval orbit means it sometimes looks too small to cover the whole Sun."],
    [2, "Why don't we see a solar eclipse at every new moon?", ["The Moon is moving too fast to line up most months","The Moon only passes in front of the Sun twice a year","Earth's rotation keeps pulling the Moon out of line","The Moon's orbit is tilted, so it usually misses the Sun"], 3, "A roughly five-degree tilt means the alignment only works near the points where the orbits cross."],
    [3, "Why does the Moon often turn red during a total lunar eclipse?", ["Earth's air bends reddened sunset light onto it","Red dust on the Moon reflects the shadow's light","The Moon heats up and glows red in the shadow","Light reflects from Mars onto its surface"], 0, "Blue light is scattered out as sunlight passes through our air, leaving red light to reach the Moon."],
    [4, "When is it safe to look at a solar eclipse without eye protection?", ["During the partial phases, if most of the Sun is covered","Only during totality, when the Sun is completely covered","When wearing dark sunglasses","Any time near sunset"], 1, "Any sliver of exposed Sun can damage the retina painlessly, so only totality is safe."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"For a few minutes at noon, stars appear. It hinges on a lucky ratio of 400."});

WB.L("comets", "space", "beginner", "Comets: dirty snowballs from the deep",
  "For thousands of years comets were seen as omens of doom. Then one astronomer predicted a comet's return, died before he could see it, and was proved right.",
  [
    ["Omens in the sky", "Before science explained them, comets were widely feared as signs of disaster, and even Aristotle misjudged what they were.",
      "A comet appears suddenly, hangs in the sky for weeks with a ghostly tail, then fades. To many ancient cultures that unpredictability was frightening. Chinese astronomers kept careful comet records for more than two thousand years, often linking them to political upheaval. In 1066 a bright comet appeared over Europe shortly before the Norman conquest of England, and the Bayeux Tapestry shows people pointing at it in alarm.\n\nThe word comet comes from the Greek for 'long-haired', describing the streaming tail. Aristotle believed comets were something happening in Earth's upper atmosphere, not objects travelling through space. It took many centuries, and better telescopes, to prove him wrong."],
    ["Halley's bold prediction", "Edmond Halley realised that several historical comets were one object returning, and predicted its comeback decades ahead.",
      "In the early 1700s, the English astronomer Edmond Halley studied records of bright comets and noticed that those seen in 1531, 1607 and 1682 had remarkably similar paths. Using his friend Isaac Newton's new laws of gravity, he argued they were a single comet on a long oval orbit, returning roughly every 75 to 76 years. He predicted it would reappear around 1758.\n\nHalley died in 1742 and never saw the result. On Christmas night 1758, a German farmer and amateur astronomer, Johann Palitzsch, spotted the comet right on cue. It was a triumph for Newton's physics, and the comet has carried Halley's name ever since. It last passed in 1986 and is due back in 2061."],
    ["A dirty snowball", "A comet's core is a small, dark lump of ice, dust and rock, and the glorious display comes from the cloud it releases.",
      "In 1950 the American astronomer Fred Whipple proposed that a comet's heart, its nucleus, is a 'dirty snowball' of frozen water, carbon dioxide, ammonia and other ices mixed with dust and rock. Space missions have since confirmed the basic idea, though many comets turned out to be more dust than ice.\n\nA typical nucleus is only a few kilometres wide and surprisingly dark, reflecting about as much light as charcoal. When the European probe Giotto flew past Halley's Comet in 1986, it photographed a lumpy, peanut-shaped nucleus roughly 15 kilometres long, jetting gas from its sunlit side. The display we see from Earth comes not from this tiny core but from the vast cloud it releases when warmed."],
    ["Two tails, not one", "A comet grows a curved dust tail and a straight gas tail, and both point away from the Sun, not behind the comet.",
      "Far from the Sun a comet is frozen and inactive. As it swings closer, sunlight warms the surface and the ices turn straight into gas, dragging dust out with them. This builds a fuzzy atmosphere called the coma, which can be larger than Jupiter.\n\nThen the tails form. The dust tail is pushed gently by sunlight, curves along the comet's path and shines yellowish white. The ion tail is made of electrically charged gas swept away by the solar wind, so it runs straight and often glows blue.\n\nHere is the surprise: both tails point away from the Sun, not behind the comet. When a comet heads back out into the solar system, it travels tail first."],
    ["Origins and Rosetta", "Comets are frozen leftovers from the solar system's birth, and in 2014 Rosetta's Philae became the first lander to touch one.",
      "Comets are leftovers from the formation of the solar system about 4.6 billion years ago. Those returning in under 200 years mostly come from the Kuiper Belt, a ring of icy bodies beyond Neptune. Comets with orbits of thousands or millions of years are thought to come from the Oort Cloud, a vast shell of icy objects. No one has directly seen it; it is inferred from the orbits of comets.\n\nThe European Space Agency's Rosetta reached comet 67P, a rubber-duck-shaped body about four kilometres long, after a ten-year chase. In November 2014 it released the Philae lander. Its harpoons failed to fire, and it bounced twice into a cliff's shadow, yet sent data for about 60 hours. Rosetta found 67P's water differs chemically from Earth's oceans."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did Aristotle wrongly believe about comets?", ["That they were distant stars like the others","That they were pieces broken off the Moon","That they were a phenomenon of Earth's atmosphere","That they were fixed in the sky all year"], 2, "Better telescopes and observation later showed that comets travel through space."],
    [1, "Why was Halley's prediction such a triumph for science?", ["It proved comets are born inside Earth's atmosphere","It showed comets never return once they have passed","It proved comets are fragments of the Moon","Newton's laws predicted its return after Halley's death"], 3, "The comet's return showed that gravity governs comets, as it does planets."],
    [2, "What is a comet's nucleus mostly made of?", ["Molten rock and metal","Ice, dust and rock","Glowing gas","Pure water ice"], 1, "The nucleus is a small, dark mix of ices, dust and rock; the bright show comes from the cloud it releases."],
    [3, "Which way do a comet's tails point?", ["Always behind the comet along its path","Towards the Sun","Towards Earth","Away from the Sun"], 3, "Sunlight and the solar wind push the tails away from the Sun, so an outbound comet travels tail first."],
    [4, "Where are long-period comets thought to come from?", ["The asteroid belt","The Kuiper Belt only","The Oort Cloud","Jupiter's moons"], 2, "The Oort Cloud is a vast shell of icy objects, known only from the orbits of the comets it sends inwards."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Two tails, yet a comet can fly tail first. How does that work?"});

WB.L("voyager-probes", "space", "beginner", "Voyager: the longest journey",
  "Two spacecraft launched in 1977 with a tiny fraction of a modern smartphone's computing power are still talking to Earth from interstellar space.",
  [
    ["A once-in-175-years chance", "A rare lineup of the outer planets let one spacecraft swing from planet to planet, using each one's gravity as a slingshot.",
      "In the 1960s, NASA engineer Gary Flandro noticed that in the late 1970s Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus and Neptune would line up in a way that happens only about once every 175 years. A spacecraft could fly past one planet and use its gravity to slingshot towards the next, saving years of travel and enormous amounts of fuel. This 'gravity assist' turned an impossible grand tour into a practical one.\n\nNASA built two identical spacecraft, Voyager 1 and Voyager 2. Confusingly, Voyager 2 launched first, on 20 August 1977, followed by Voyager 1 on 5 September. Voyager 1 was on a faster path, so it overtook its twin and reached Jupiter first."],
    ["Revealing new worlds", "The Voyagers turned the giant planets' moons from dots of light into worlds, and Voyager 2 alone visited Uranus and Neptune.",
      "Before the Voyagers, the giant planets' moons were little more than dots of light. At Jupiter in 1979, the probes found active volcanoes on the moon Io, the first ever seen beyond Earth. They imaged Europa's cracked icy crust, hinting at an ocean beneath, and found thin rings around Jupiter. At Saturn in 1980 and 1981 they revealed intricate structure in the rings and studied the hazy moon Titan.\n\nVoyager 1 was then bent up and out of the plane of the planets for a close look at Titan. Voyager 2 carried on alone, becoming the only spacecraft ever to visit Uranus, in 1986, and Neptune, in 1989."],
    ["Pale Blue Dot, golden record", "Voyager 1 photographed Earth as a speck from six billion kilometres, and each Voyager carries a golden record of life on Earth.",
      "On 14 February 1990, at the urging of astronomer Carl Sagan, Voyager 1 turned its camera back towards home from about six billion kilometres away. Earth appears as a speck less than a pixel wide, suspended in a beam of sunlight. The photo became famous for its perspective, not its detail. Soon afterwards the cameras were switched off for good to save power.\n\nEach Voyager also carries a gold-plated copper record, with contents chosen by a committee chaired by Sagan. It holds greetings in 55 languages, sounds of rain, surf, whales and a mother's kiss, 115 images, and music from Bach to Chuck Berry. The cover shows how to play it and the Sun's position among pulsars. Finding it is very unlikely; it is partly a message to ourselves."],
    ["Into interstellar space", "Voyager 1 crossed the heliopause into interstellar space in 2012 and Voyager 2 followed in 2018, though both remain under the Sun's gravity.",
      "The Sun blows a constant stream of charged particles, the solar wind, which inflates a vast bubble around the solar system called the heliosphere. Its outer boundary, the heliopause, is where the solar wind gives way to the thin gas between the stars.\n\nIn August 2012 Voyager 1 crossed that boundary, becoming the first human-made object to reach interstellar space. Voyager 2 crossed in November 2018. Neither has left the solar system in every sense, because the Sun's gravity still rules a region far beyond, including the Oort Cloud of comets.\n\nVoyager 1 is now more than 24 billion kilometres away, so far that its radio signals take nearly a full day to reach us."],
    ["Kept alive from Canberra", "Engineers keep the Voyagers running by rationing power and patching old software, and only the Canberra dish can command Voyager 2.",
      "Each Voyager runs on generators that turn the heat of decaying plutonium into electricity. Output drops by a few watts a year, so engineers have switched off heaters and instruments one by one. The transmitters use roughly the power of a refrigerator light bulb. In 2023 Voyager 1 began sending gibberish because of a failed memory chip. A small team spent months diagnosing it from over 20 light-hours away, moved the affected code elsewhere, and restored contact in 2024.\n\nAfter Neptune, Voyager 2 was flung south, out of view of the Deep Space Network's stations in California and Spain. Only the 70-metre dish at Tidbinbilla near Canberra can send it commands. When that dish was offline for upgrades in 2020, Voyager 2 could be heard but not commanded for months."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What made the Voyagers' grand tour of the outer planets practical?", ["A new kind of nuclear rocket engine","A rare planetary alignment allowing gravity assists","Refuelling stops at Jupiter and Saturn","A launch from a base on the Moon"], 1, "Each planet's gravity flung the craft onward, saving years of travel and huge amounts of fuel."],
    [1, "Which planets has only Voyager 2 visited?", ["Jupiter and Saturn","Mars and Venus","Pluto and Neptune","Uranus and Neptune"], 3, "Voyager 2 continued alone after Saturn, reaching Uranus in 1986 and Neptune in 1989."],
    [2, "Why is the Pale Blue Dot photograph so famous?", ["It shows how small Earth looks from deep space","It was the sharpest image of Earth ever taken","It was the first picture of Earth's continents","It showed signs of life on Earth"], 0, "Its power lies in the perspective: everyone who has ever lived spent their life on that speck."],
    [3, "Why is Voyager 1 not considered to have left the solar system entirely?", ["Its radio signals can still be received on Earth","The Sun's gravity still dominates a region far beyond","It remains inside the heliosphere bubble","It is still in orbit around Neptune"], 1, "Crossing the heliopause means leaving the solar wind's bubble, not the Sun's gravitational reach."],
    [4, "Why can only the Canberra station send commands to Voyager 2?", ["It has the oldest dish of the three stations","The other two stations have been permanently switched off","It headed south, out of view of the northern stations","Voyager 2 was built and launched in Australia"], 2, "Voyager 2 sits deep in the southern sky, where only the Tidbinbilla dish can see it and transmit."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Still talking after nearly 50 years, on less power than a fridge bulb."});

WB.L("venus", "space", "beginner", "Venus: Earth's hellish twin",
  "It is almost the same size as Earth and the brightest planet in our sky. Land on it, though, and you would be crushed, cooked and dissolved within hours.",
  [
    ["The morning and evening star", "Venus is the brightest natural object in the night sky after the Moon, and it only appears around twilight.",
      "If you have ever noticed a brilliant 'star' low in the west after sunset or in the east before dawn, it was probably Venus. It orbits closer to the Sun than we do, so it never strays far from the Sun in our sky and shows only around twilight.\n\nIts thick, bright clouds reflect about three quarters of the sunlight that hits them, making it dazzling. Ancient peoples sometimes thought the morning and evening appearances were two different objects; the Greeks called them Phosphorus and Hesperus before realising they were the same planet. Venus is so bright that it is regularly reported as a UFO, and under very dark skies it can even cast a faint shadow."],
    ["Twin on the outside", "Venus is nearly Earth's size and mass, but its dense carbon dioxide atmosphere and acid clouds made it a completely different world.",
      "On paper, Venus looks like Earth's sibling. Its diameter is about 95 percent of Earth's, its mass about 80 percent, and it is made of similar rock. For a long time people imagined steamy swamps or oceans under its permanent clouds, and early science fiction filled it with jungles. The truth only emerged with radio telescopes and spacecraft in the 1960s.\n\nVenus's atmosphere is about 96 percent carbon dioxide, and so dense that surface pressure is around 90 times Earth's, like being nearly a kilometre deep in the ocean. Above sit thick clouds of sulfuric acid droplets. Whatever the two planets shared at birth, they took very different paths."],
    ["Hotter than Mercury", "A runaway greenhouse effect makes Venus's surface about 465°C, hotter than Mercury and hot enough to melt lead.",
      "Mercury is much closer to the Sun, yet Venus is the hottest planet in the solar system. Its surface sits at roughly 465°C, day and night, from pole to equator. The cause is a runaway greenhouse effect. Sunlight that gets through the clouds warms the ground, but the dense carbon dioxide traps the heat trying to escape to space.\n\nMany scientists think Venus may once have been cooler, possibly with shallow oceans. As the young Sun brightened, the water evaporated, adding more heat-trapping vapour until the oceans were lost. That history is still debated, but Venus shows how powerfully an atmosphere can control a planet's temperature."],
    ["Longer than a year", "Venus spins backwards and so slowly that one rotation takes longer than one orbit of the Sun.",
      "Venus takes about 225 Earth days to orbit the Sun but about 243 to spin once on its axis, so its day is longer than its year. It also rotates in the opposite direction to most planets, so the Sun would rise in the west and set in the east, if you could see it through the clouds.\n\nNobody is certain why. Ideas include a giant collision early in its history, or the slow effect of the Sun's tides on its heavy atmosphere. Oddly, while the planet itself turns at a crawl, its upper atmosphere races around the whole planet in about four Earth days, driven by hurricane-force winds."],
    ["Landers and a transit", "Soviet Venera probes landed on Venus and sent back pictures, and a Venus transit in 1769 sent Captain Cook towards Australia.",
      "Landing on Venus is brutally hard, yet the Soviet Union did it repeatedly. Venera 7 sent faint signals from the surface in December 1970, the first data from the surface of another planet. In 1975 Venera 9 and 10 returned the first pictures of the ground. In 1982 Venera 13 sent colour images and survived about two hours. NASA's Magellan orbiter later mapped almost the whole planet by radar through the clouds, revealing volcanoes and lava plains.\n\nOccasionally Venus crosses the Sun's face, a transit. Timing one from distant places could reveal the Earth-Sun distance, so Britain sent Lieutenant James Cook to Tahiti for the June 1769 transit. His sealed orders then led to New Zealand and, in 1770, Australia's east coast. The next transit is in 2117."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why is Venus only seen around dawn or dusk?", ["It only reflects enough sunlight at those times","Earth's shadow hides it for most of the night","Its orbit is inside Earth's, so it stays near the Sun","It moves too fast to be seen later in the night"], 2, "A planet inside Earth's orbit can never appear far from the Sun in our sky."],
    [1, "What is Venus's atmosphere mostly made of?", ["Nitrogen","Carbon dioxide","Oxygen","Hydrogen"], 1, "A dense carbon dioxide atmosphere, with acid clouds above, makes Venus very unlike Earth."],
    [2, "Why is Venus hotter than Mercury, which is closer to the Sun?", ["Its core is much hotter than Mercury's","It has more volcanoes than any other planet","It is closer to the Sun than Earth is","A runaway greenhouse effect traps its heat"], 3, "Dense carbon dioxide traps heat, so atmosphere matters more than distance here."],
    [3, "What is unusual about Venus's rotation?", ["It spins backwards, so its day outlasts its year","It spins faster than any other planet does","It rotates once per orbit, always facing the Sun","It rotates on its side, like Uranus does"], 0, "A turn takes about 243 Earth days, but an orbit takes only about 225."],
    [4, "Why did Britain send James Cook to Tahiti in 1769?", ["To claim Tahiti for the British Crown before France","To trade for spices and provisions with the islanders","To observe the transit of Venus, to measure the Sun's distance","To map the coast of South America for the navy"], 2, "Timing the transit from far-apart places let astronomers measure the Sun's distance."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Earth's twin has a day that lasts longer than its year."});

WB.L("pluto-dwarf-planets", "space", "beginner", "Pluto and the dwarf planets",
  "In 2006 Pluto was voted out of the planet club. The real story is less about Pluto being demoted and more about discovering it had a lot of company.",
  [
    ["Found by patience", "Clyde Tombaugh found Pluto in 1930 by comparing photographs of the sky, partly through luck and mostly through patience.",
      "In the early 1900s, astronomer Percival Lowell was convinced an unknown 'Planet X' lay beyond Neptune, and his observatory in Flagstaff, Arizona, kept searching after his death. In 1929 it hired Clyde Tombaugh, a 22-year-old Kansas farm boy with no university degree, who had impressed staff with drawings from his homemade telescope.\n\nTombaugh photographed the same patches of sky several nights apart and flicked between the plates with a blink comparator, looking for a dot that moved against the background stars. On 18 February 1930 he found one. The calculations that inspired the hunt later proved wrong, so finding Pluto was partly luck, combined with enormous patience."],
    ["Named by a schoolgirl", "The name Pluto was suggested by 11-year-old Venetia Burney from Oxford, and it honoured Percival Lowell too.",
      "News of the new planet spread worldwide, and name suggestions poured in. In Oxford, England, 11-year-old Venetia Burney heard about it over breakfast and suggested Pluto, the Roman god of the underworld, fitting for a cold, dark world at the edge of the solar system. Her grandfather passed the idea to an astronomer friend, who sent it to the Lowell Observatory.\n\nStaff there voted unanimously in favour, partly because the first two letters, P and L, honoured Percival Lowell. Venetia reportedly received a five-pound note as a reward. Soon after, Walt Disney introduced a cartoon dog named Pluto. Decades later, an instrument on the New Horizons spacecraft was named in her honour."],
    ["Smaller and smaller", "Each new measurement shrank Pluto, until it proved far smaller than first thought, and smaller even than our Moon.",
      "At discovery, Pluto was assumed to be roughly Earth-sized, because it was thought to be disturbing Neptune's orbit. Each new measurement shrank it. In 1978 astronomers discovered its largest moon, Charon, which allowed Pluto's mass to be calculated precisely. It was tiny: only about a fifth of our Moon's mass.\n\nPluto is about 2,380 kilometres across, narrower than Australia is wide. Charon is about half Pluto's diameter, so large that the two swing around a shared point in the space between them. Pluto's orbit is also odd: stretched and tilted, and between 1979 and 1999 it came closer to the Sun than Neptune did."],
    ["A crowded neighbourhood", "Pluto is one of the largest members of the Kuiper Belt, and the discovery of Eris forced a formal definition of a planet.",
      "In 1992 astronomers found another small icy body beyond Neptune, the first known member of what is now called the Kuiper Belt. Hundreds, then thousands more followed. Pluto was not a lonely ninth planet but one of the largest of a vast belt of frozen leftovers.\n\nThe crisis came in 2005, when a team led by Mike Brown announced Eris, first thought larger than Pluto. Later measurements showed it is very slightly smaller in diameter but about a quarter more massive. If Pluto was a planet, Eris had to be one too, and possibly dozens more.\n\nIn August 2006 the International Astronomical Union defined a planet in Prague: it orbits the Sun, is massive enough to be round, and has cleared the neighbourhood around its orbit."],
    ["Pluto has a heart", "Pluto failed the 'cleared its neighbourhood' test and became a dwarf planet, yet New Horizons found a surprisingly active world.",
      "Pluto passes the first two tests but fails the third, because it shares its region with countless Kuiper Belt objects. It became a dwarf planet, alongside Eris and the asteroid Ceres, with Haumea and Makemake added later. Some planetary scientists still argue for a definition based on a body's own properties, not its surroundings.\n\nNASA's New Horizons flew past Pluto on 14 July 2015, nine and a half years after launch. Expectations were modest: an old, cratered ball of ice. Instead it found a pale heart-shaped region, Tombaugh Regio, holding Sputnik Planitia, a young plain of nitrogen ice that flows like glaciers and has almost no craters. There were also water-ice mountains kilometres high and a blue haze. Some of Tombaugh's ashes were aboard."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How did Clyde Tombaugh find Pluto?", ["Using a large radio telescope in Arizona","By mathematical predictions from Lowell alone","By comparing photographs for a dot that moved","By sending a space probe out beyond Neptune"], 2, "Careful, patient comparison of photographic plates revealed a moving object."],
    [1, "Why was the name Pluto chosen?", ["It honoured the farm where the discoverer grew up","A fitting Roman god, and P and L honour Percival Lowell","It was named after a cartoon dog from a Disney film","It was chosen by a public vote held in Britain"], 1, "The Lowell staff liked the god's fit and the initials of Percival Lowell."],
    [2, "What let astronomers measure Pluto's mass precisely and show it was tiny?", ["Landing a probe on it","Its eclipse of Neptune","The discovery of its moon Charon in 1978","The 2006 planet vote"], 2, "A moon's orbit reveals the mass of the body it circles, and Pluto proved far smaller than expected."],
    [3, "Why did the discovery of Eris force a new definition of a planet?", ["Eris was discovered to be larger than Neptune","If Pluto were a planet, Eris and others would be too","Eris turned out not to orbit the Sun at all","Eris was found inside the orbit of Earth"], 1, "Pluto is one of many Kuiper Belt worlds, so a clear rule was needed."],
    [4, "Which test of the 2006 planet definition does Pluto fail?", ["Orbiting the Sun","Being roughly round in shape","Having no large moons","Clearing the neighbourhood around its orbit"], 3, "Pluto shares its region with countless Kuiper Belt objects, so it does not dominate its path."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Pluto lost its planet status, but not for the reason most people think."});

WB.L("jupiter-saturn", "space", "intermediate", "Jupiter and Saturn: the giants",
  "Jupiter could swallow every other planet with room to spare, and Saturn is light enough, in a sense, to float. Meet the two kings of the solar system.",
  [
    ["King of the planets", "Jupiter has more than twice the mass of all the other planets combined, and its moons helped overturn the Earth-centred universe.",
      "Jupiter is about 11 times wider than Earth and roughly 318 times more massive. Put all the other planets together and Jupiter still outweighs them more than twice over. Despite its size, it spins faster than any other planet, a day lasting just under 10 hours, which visibly flattens it at the poles.\n\nWhen Galileo pointed his telescope at it in January 1610, he saw four small points of light that changed position night after night. They were moons circling Jupiter: Io, Europa, Ganymede and Callisto. It was powerful evidence that not everything in the heavens orbits Earth, and it helped undermine the old Earth-centred view. Ganymede is larger than the planet Mercury."],
    ["Not a failed star", "Jupiter is not a failed star, since it would need roughly 80 times more mass to ignite fusion.",
      "Jupiter is often called a 'failed star' because it is mostly hydrogen and helium, like the Sun. The label misleads. To ignite hydrogen fusion in its core, an object needs roughly 80 times Jupiter's mass, and Jupiter was never close. It gives off more heat than it receives from the Sun, left over from its formation and slow contraction, but it does not shine by fusion.\n\nIts interior is extraordinary all the same. Under crushing pressure, hydrogen is thought to become an electrically conducting fluid called metallic hydrogen. Swirling currents in it probably generate Jupiter's enormous magnetic field, the strongest of any planet, which traps radiation lethal to unshielded spacecraft electronics."],
    ["The Great Red Spot", "Jupiter's Great Red Spot is a storm wider than Earth that has been tracked for about two centuries, and it reaches hundreds of kilometres deep.",
      "Jupiter's colourful stripes are bands of cloud driven by powerful jet streams flowing east and west. Its most famous feature is the Great Red Spot, a high-pressure storm that spins anticlockwise and is wider than Earth. Astronomers have tracked it continuously since at least the 1830s. Giovanni Cassini described a spot in 1665, but historians are unsure whether it was the same storm.\n\nThe spot has been shrinking over the past century and has become rounder, though it is still enormous. NASA's Juno spacecraft, orbiting Jupiter since 2016, has measured that the storm reaches hundreds of kilometres down into the atmosphere. Nobody is quite sure why it has lasted so long or what gives it its reddish colour."],
    ["Rings and a floating planet", "Saturn's rings are mostly water ice, vast but very thin, and Saturn is the only planet less dense than water.",
      "Saturn's rings are countless pieces of ice, from tiny grains to house-sized chunks, each orbiting like a miniature moon. The main rings span roughly 280,000 kilometres, about three quarters of the distance to the Moon, yet are often only about ten metres thick. Galileo saw them in 1610 but his blurry telescope made him think Saturn had 'ears'; Christiaan Huygens worked out in the 1650s that they were a ring. Whether they formed with Saturn or are much younger is still debated.\n\nSaturn's average density is about 0.7 grams per cubic centimetre, less than water's 1.0, so it would float in a big enough bathtub. The idea falls apart quickly, since Saturn has no solid surface. Its low density and fast spin make it the most flattened planet. At its north pole sits a six-sided jet stream wider than Earth."],
    ["Cassini's Grand Finale", "Cassini studied Saturn for 13 years, then plunged into it deliberately in 2017 to protect moons that might host life.",
      "The Cassini-Huygens mission, a partnership between NASA, the European Space Agency and the Italian Space Agency, arrived at Saturn in 2004. Its Huygens probe parachuted onto Titan in January 2005, the most distant landing ever made, revealing riverbeds and lakes of liquid methane and ethane. Cassini also found jets of water vapour spraying from the small moon Enceladus, evidence of a hidden ocean.\n\nBy 2017 the spacecraft was almost out of fuel. To avoid any risk of it crashing into, and contaminating, a moon that might host life, controllers steered it through the gap between Saturn and its rings 22 times. Then, on 15 September 2017, they sent it plunging into Saturn's atmosphere. It transmitted data until the very end."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why were the four moons Galileo saw around Jupiter so important?", ["They proved Jupiter had a set of rings","They showed not everything orbits Earth","They proved Jupiter was really a small star","They revealed the Great Red Spot"], 1, "Moons circling Jupiter undermined the idea that everything orbits Earth."],
    [1, "Why is calling Jupiter a 'failed star' misleading?", ["It is made mostly of rock and metal, not gas","It was once a star that burned itself out","It would need about 80 times more mass to ignite","It is far too cold to ever shine at all"], 2, "Jupiter was never close to the mass needed for fusion."],
    [2, "What is Jupiter's Great Red Spot?", ["A giant storm wider than Earth","A shadow cast by one of its moons","A giant volcano that erupts on its surface","A huge crater left by an old impact"], 0, "It is a high-pressure storm that reaches hundreds of kilometres down."],
    [3, "What is unusual about Saturn's average density?", ["It is the densest planet","It is greater than Earth's","It equals Jupiter's","It is lower than water's"], 3, "Saturn is mostly hydrogen and helium, so it is less dense than water, though it has no solid surface to float."],
    [4, "Why was Cassini deliberately sent into Saturn?", ["To test a new engine in Saturn's atmosphere","To protect moons that might host life","Because it had lost contact with Earth","To photograph Saturn's core up close"], 1, "Controllers wanted to eliminate any risk of crashing into a moon such as Enceladus or Titan."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"One of the giants is light enough, in a sense, to float. Which one?"});

WB.L("planetary-defence", "space", "intermediate", "Planetary defence: dodging asteroids",
  "The dinosaurs had no space program. We do, and in 2022 humanity knocked an asteroid off course for the very first time.",
  [
    ["Earth is still hit", "Space rocks strike Earth all the time, and an asteroid impact is the one natural disaster we could see coming years ahead and prevent.",
      "Tonnes of space dust and small rocks fall on Earth every day, mostly burning up as shooting stars. Larger impacts are rarer but real. About 66 million years ago an asteroid roughly ten kilometres wide struck near Mexico's Yucatán Peninsula, and the resulting global catastrophe wiped out the non-bird dinosaurs. Impacts on that scale are thought to happen only every hundred million years or so. Smaller ones are far more frequent and can still devastate a city or region.\n\nThe good news is that, of all natural disasters, an asteroid impact is the only one we can, in principle, see coming years ahead and prevent entirely. That is the job of planetary defence."],
    ["Tunguska and Chelyabinsk", "Airbursts over Russia in 1908 and 2013 show what relatively small asteroids can do, and what to do if you see a fireball.",
      "On 30 June 1908, something exploded in the air above Tunguska in Siberia, flattening an estimated 80 million trees across about 2,000 square kilometres. Most scientists think it was a stony asteroid or comet fragment perhaps 50 to 60 metres across. The area was so remote that very few people were hurt.\n\nOn 15 February 2013, a rock about 20 metres wide burst over the Russian city of Chelyabinsk. Its blast wave arrived well after the flash, shattering windows across the city, and about 1,500 people were injured, mostly by flying glass. Many had rushed to windows to look.\n\nExperts now advise treating a very bright fireball like lightning: move away from windows and wait for the boom. Such events are extremely rare. If anyone is injured, call 000."],
    ["Watching the skies", "Survey telescopes have found over 90 percent of the largest near-Earth asteroids, and none is on a collision course.",
      "Since the 1990s, telescopes have systematically scanned the sky for near-Earth objects, asteroids and comets whose orbits bring them close to ours. Surveys such as Catalina, Pan-STARRS and ATLAS photograph the sky night after night, looking for moving dots, and astronomers then calculate each orbit decades ahead.\n\nScientists believe more than 90 percent of near-Earth asteroids larger than a kilometre, the kind that could cause global disaster, have been found, and none poses a known threat for at least the next century. Smaller objects, still big enough to destroy a city, are far less complete. New facilities, including the Vera C. Rubin Observatory in Chile and planned space telescopes, should find many more."],
    ["False alarms and Apophis", "New asteroids sometimes show a small impact chance that vanishes as their orbits are measured more precisely.",
      "When an asteroid is first spotted, its path is uncertain, like predicting where a thrown ball will land from a split-second glimpse. Sometimes Earth falls inside the early range of possibilities, so the odds look worrying, then drop to zero as observations accumulate.\n\nIn 2004 the asteroid Apophis, about 340 metres wide, briefly showed a chance of nearly 3 percent of hitting Earth in 2029. Further tracking ruled out any impact in 2029 and for at least the next century. Instead, on 13 April 2029 it will pass within about 32,000 kilometres of Earth, closer than many communications satellites, and may be visible to the naked eye from parts of the world. In early 2025, asteroid 2024 YR4 followed a similar pattern before being ruled out as a threat to Earth."],
    ["DART: the first nudge", "In 2022 NASA deliberately crashed a spacecraft into an asteroid moonlet and measurably changed its orbit.",
      "Finding a dangerous asteroid is only half the problem; we also need a way to move it. The simplest idea is a kinetic impactor: hit it hard with a spacecraft, years ahead, so a tiny change in speed grows into a miss by the time it reaches Earth.\n\nNASA's Double Asteroid Redirection Test, or DART, tried this on a harmless target. Dimorphos is a moonlet about 160 metres wide, orbiting a larger asteroid called Didymos. On 26 September 2022, DART slammed into it at over 20,000 kilometres per hour. Dimorphos had orbited Didymos in about 11 hours and 55 minutes; afterwards its orbit was about 32 minutes shorter, far more than the minimum mission goal. The European Space Agency's Hera mission is following up."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why is an asteroid impact unlike other natural disasters?", ["It is far more frequent than any other disaster","It always destroys a whole continent","It can be seen coming years in advance","It only ever happens over the oceans"], 2, "We can find and track asteroids long before they arrive, which makes prevention possible in principle."],
    [1, "If you see an extremely bright fireball, what is sensible advice?", ["Rush to the window for a better look","Go outside straight away to find the meteorite","Stay clear of windows until any blast passes","Switch off the power at the meter box"], 2, "The blast arrives well after the flash, and flying glass injured most people at Chelyabinsk."],
    [2, "Roughly what share of near-Earth asteroids over one kilometre wide have been found?", ["More than 90 percent","About half","About 10 percent","Almost none so far"], 0, "The biggest threats are well mapped, though smaller city-destroying objects are far less complete."],
    [3, "Why do some newly found asteroids show a worrying impact chance that later vanishes?", ["Their orbits shift suddenly while they travel","Early data leaves the path uncertain until refined","They hide behind the Sun for several months","They break apart as they near Earth"], 1, "Early path estimates have a wide range, and more observations usually shrink it until Earth falls outside."],
    [4, "What did the DART mission achieve?", ["It landed astronauts on an asteroid","It destroyed a dangerous asteroid in space","It brought back samples from an asteroid","It shifted the orbit of an asteroid moonlet"], 3, "A deliberate impact shortened Dimorphos's orbit by about 32 minutes, proving the technique can work."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Humanity moved a space rock on purpose once. It was easier than expected."});

WB.L("australia-in-space", "space", "intermediate", "Australia's place in space",
  "A sheep paddock dish carried Apollo 11's moonwalk to the world, and a quiet desert range launched a satellite in 1967. Australia's space story is bigger than most people know.",
  [
    ["Woomera and WRESAT", "Woomera, a remote rocket range in South Australia, launched Australia's first satellite, WRESAT, in 1967.",
      "In 1947, Britain and Australia agreed to build a long-range weapons testing site in the South Australian outback. The town of Woomera, named after an Aboriginal spear-thrower, grew up to support it. The range stretched well over a thousand kilometres across the desert, giving room to fire rockets far from populated areas.\n\nIn the mid-1960s the United States offered Australia a spare Redstone rocket. Scientists in Salisbury, South Australia, built a small satellite, WRESAT, in under a year. It launched from Woomera on 29 November 1967, making Australia one of the first handful of nations to launch a satellite from its own territory. It measured the Sun's radiation and the upper atmosphere for a few days, then burned up in January 1968. Britain's only satellite on a British rocket, Prospero, followed from Woomera in 1971."],
    ["The Dish at Parkes", "The 64-metre Parkes radio telescope supplied most of the television pictures of the Apollo 11 moonwalk.",
      "The Parkes radio telescope opened in 1961 among the sheep paddocks of central western New South Wales, its 64-metre dish among the most advanced in the world. In July 1969, when Neil Armstrong stepped onto the Moon, it was high over Australia.\n\nThe first minutes of television were received by NASA's Honeysuckle Creek station near Canberra, which captured Armstrong climbing down the ladder. Parkes then took over and supplied the pictures for most of the roughly two-and-a-half-hour moonwalk, despite strong winds that pushed the dish close to its safety limits. The 2000 film The Dish dramatised the story, with some liberties. In 2020 the telescope also received the Wiradjuri name Murriyang."],
    ["Listening to the deep", "Canberra hosts one of NASA's three Deep Space Network stations, and Parkes found the first fast radio burst in its archives.",
      "To keep in touch with distant spacecraft as Earth rotates, NASA built three tracking stations roughly a third of the way around the globe from one another: Goldstone in California, Madrid in Spain, and Tidbinbilla near Canberra. At any moment at least one can see a given spacecraft.\n\nThe Canberra Deep Space Communication Complex, run on NASA's behalf by Australia's national science agency CSIRO, has helped communicate with missions such as Voyager, the Mars rovers and New Horizons.\n\nParkes has made discoveries of its own. In 2007, astronomers searching its archived data found the first known fast radio burst, a millisecond flash of radio waves from far beyond our galaxy, opening up an entirely new field of astronomy."],
    ["The Skylab fine", "When Skylab fell over Western Australia in 1979, the local shire jokingly fined NASA for littering.",
      "Skylab, America's first space station, re-entered the atmosphere in July 1979, earlier than planned, and broke up over the Indian Ocean and southern Western Australia. Debris rained down around the town of Esperance and across the Nullarbor, though no one was hurt.\n\nThe Shire of Esperance issued NASA a light-hearted 400-dollar fine for littering. A San Francisco newspaper offered 10,000 dollars for the first piece of Skylab delivered to its office. It was claimed by a 17-year-old from Esperance, Stan Thornton, who flew to the United States with fragments.\n\nThe fine went unpaid for 30 years, until a Californian radio host raised the money from listeners and paid it in 2009."],
    ["Australians in orbit", "Australians have flown with NASA, and Australia now has its own space agency and a landing site for asteroid samples.",
      "Paul Scully-Power, an Australian-born oceanographer, flew aboard the Space Shuttle Challenger in 1984 as a payload specialist. Adelaide-born engineer Andy Thomas became a NASA astronaut and flew four missions, including a long stay on the Russian space station Mir in 1998.\n\nIn 2024 Katherine Bennell-Pegg became the first astronaut to graduate from training under the Australian flag, completing the European Space Agency's basic astronaut training. Australia established its own Australian Space Agency in 2018.\n\nIts deserts remain useful too. In 2010 and 2020, Japanese Hayabusa capsules carrying samples from asteroids parachuted down near Woomera, bringing pieces of other worlds to Australian soil. The remote outback that suited rocket tests also suits recovering spacecraft."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why was Woomera chosen as a rocket range?", ["It was close to Sydney and its workforce","It was the coldest place in Australia","It already had a large jet airport","Its remote location gave room to fire rockets safely"], 3, "Vast, empty desert let rockets be tested far from populated areas."],
    [1, "What role did Parkes play in the Apollo 11 broadcast?", ["It supplied pictures for most of the moonwalk","It sent the landing commands to the crew","It tracked the Saturn V rocket after launch","It recorded the astronauts' heart rates"], 0, "Honeysuckle Creek caught the first minutes; Parkes then carried most of the moonwalk to the world."],
    [2, "Why does NASA have three Deep Space Network stations around the globe?", ["Each one is assigned to a different planet","So one can always see a spacecraft as Earth rotates","Three stations cost less than a single large one","To follow different spacecraft in different seasons"], 1, "Spacing them about a third of the way round keeps every spacecraft in view of one station."],
    [3, "How did the Shire of Esperance respond to Skylab debris landing in 1979?", ["It sued the United States for the damage","It built a museum and charged NASA entry","It fined NASA 400 dollars for littering","It banned future space stations from the area"], 2, "The light-hearted fine went unpaid for 30 years, until a radio host paid it in 2009."],
    [4, "Why do Japanese asteroid sample capsules land near Woomera?", ["It is the closest launch site to Japan","The remote desert is a safe, empty landing zone","Australian engineers built the capsules for Japan","The capsules were launched from Woomera originally"], 1, "Hayabusa capsules parachuted into the same remote outback that suited rocket testing."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A sheep paddock dish helped the world watch a moonwalk. Here's the story."});

WB.L("spectroscopy-starlight", "space", "intermediate", "Reading starlight: what stars are made of",
  "In 1835 a famous philosopher declared that we could never know what the stars are made of. Within a few decades, scientists were doing exactly that, using rainbows.",
  [
    ["An impossible question", "In 1835 Auguste Comte called the chemistry of the stars forever unknowable, a reminder that 'impossible' is a dangerous word.",
      "The French philosopher Auguste Comte, founder of positivism, wanted science to stick to what could be observed. In 1835 he gave an example of knowledge forever beyond reach: we could study the stars' shapes, distances and motions, but never their chemical composition, since no one could visit them or take a sample.\n\nIt seemed reasonable. Chemistry meant handling substances in a laboratory, and the stars were unreachably far away. Yet within about 25 years, scientists found a way to identify elements in the Sun from Earth. Comte's claim is now often quoted as a warning that 'impossible' can be a dangerous word. The key had already been found, in a ray of sunlight split by glass."],
    ["Dark lines in the rainbow", "Fraunhofer found hundreds of dark lines crossing the Sun's spectrum, and Kirchhoff and Bunsen showed each element makes its own pattern.",
      "Isaac Newton showed in the 1660s that a prism splits sunlight into a rainbow, a spectrum. In 1814, German optician Joseph von Fraunhofer built instruments precise enough to magnify it and found hundreds of thin dark lines, as if exact colours were missing. He mapped about 570 and labelled the strongest with letters, a system still used. He could not explain them.\n\nAround 1859 in Heidelberg, physicist Gustav Kirchhoff and chemist Robert Bunsen heated substances in a flame and studied the light through a prism. Each element gave its own set of bright lines, like a barcode. Sodium's two yellow lines matched two of Fraunhofer's dark lines. Cooler gas in the Sun's outer layers absorbs those same colours, so dark lines reveal which elements are present."],
    ["Helium, found in sunlight", "Helium was identified in the Sun's spectrum in 1868, decades before anyone found it on Earth.",
      "During a total solar eclipse in 1868, astronomers studying the Sun's glowing outer layers saw a bright yellow line that matched no known element. Jules Janssen observed it from India, and Norman Lockyer studied it soon afterwards. Lockyer, working with chemist Edward Frankland, concluded it belonged to a new element and named it helium, after Helios, the Greek god of the Sun.\n\nMany chemists were sceptical of an element known only from a distant star. Then in 1895 Scottish chemist William Ramsay detected the same spectral line from gas released by a uranium mineral. Helium, the second most common element in the universe, had first been discovered 150 million kilometres away. The method also found two new elements on Earth, caesium and rubidium."],
    ["Cecilia Payne's discovery", "In 1925 Cecilia Payne showed that stars are made overwhelmingly of hydrogen and helium, overturning expert opinion.",
      "In the early 1900s astronomers assumed stars had about the same make-up as Earth's crust, rich in heavier elements like iron. Cecilia Payne, a young British astronomer who moved to Harvard because women had few prospects in astronomy in England, applied new atomic physics to stellar spectra.\n\nHer 1925 doctoral thesis concluded that stars are overwhelmingly hydrogen and helium, in vastly greater amounts than anyone expected. The senior astronomer Henry Norris Russell persuaded her to describe the result as almost certainly not real. A few years later Russell reached the same conclusion himself.\n\nPayne was right: hydrogen and helium make up about 98 percent of the ordinary matter in stars. Her thesis is now considered among the most important in astronomy."],
    ["Colour, temperature, motion", "A star's spectrum also reveals its temperature and whether it is moving towards or away from us.",
      "Spectra hold far more than a list of ingredients. A star's colour reveals its surface temperature: red stars are relatively cool, around 3,000°C, while blue-white stars can exceed 20,000°C. In the early 1900s, Annie Jump Cannon at Harvard classified hundreds of thousands of stars by spectrum into the sequence O, B, A, F, G, K and M, from hottest to coolest, which astronomers still use.\n\nSpectra also reveal motion through the Doppler effect. Light from an object moving away is stretched towards the red end, while light from one approaching is squeezed towards blue. Measuring these tiny shifts showed that distant galaxies are rushing away from us, the evidence for an expanding universe, and it still helps detect planets around other stars."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What lesson is Auguste Comte's 1835 claim often used to illustrate?", ["Philosophers make the best scientists","Stars are made of fire","Light travels instantly","Calling something unknowable can prove wrong"], 3, "Within about 25 years, scientists were identifying the Sun's elements from Earth."],
    [1, "What did Kirchhoff and Bunsen realise about the Sun's dark lines?", ["Each element has its own unique pattern of colours","They were flaws in the glass of the prism","They were caused only by Earth's atmosphere","They showed how fast the Sun rotates"], 0, "A star's dark lines work like a barcode revealing which elements are present."],
    [2, "Why was the discovery of helium remarkable?", ["It was first isolated from volcanic gas on Earth","It was the first element chemists made artificially","It was seen in the Sun's light decades before Earth","It was first detected in a fallen meteorite"], 2, "A mystery yellow line in sunlight pointed to a new element, which was found on Earth only in 1895."],
    [3, "What did Cecilia Payne's 1925 thesis show about stars?", ["They are mostly iron","They are made like Earth's crust","They are mostly carbon","They are overwhelmingly hydrogen and helium"], 3, "Her spectral analysis overturned the assumption that stars resemble Earth's crust."],
    [4, "What can the Doppler shift in a star's light tell us?", ["The star's age in millions of years","Whether it is moving towards or away from us","The exact mix of elements inside it","How wide the star is across"], 1, "Light stretched towards red means moving away; squeezed towards blue means approaching."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A philosopher said we'd never know what stars are made of. A rainbow disagreed."});

WB.L("dark-matter-energy", "space", "advanced", "Dark matter and dark energy",
  "Everything we have ever seen, every star, planet and person, makes up only about 5 percent of the universe. The rest is a mystery with a name but no explanation.",
  [
    ["The missing mass", "Galaxies spin and cluster as if they hold far more mass than their visible stars and gas supply.",
      "In 1933 Swiss astronomer Fritz Zwicky measured the speeds of galaxies in the Coma Cluster. They moved so fast that the cluster should have flown apart, unless it held far more mass than its light suggested. He called the hidden material 'dunkle Materie', German for dark matter. His estimate was far too large, since distances were badly underestimated then, but the puzzle was real.\n\nIn the 1970s Vera Rubin and Kent Ford measured spiral galaxies such as Andromeda. In a solar system, outer planets orbit slowly. In these galaxies the outer stars did not slow down. The simplest explanation was a vast, invisible halo of extra mass around each galaxy."],
    ["Seeing the invisible", "Gravitational lensing maps where dark matter sits, and colliding clusters show it is a real substance.",
      "Dark matter gives off no light, but it has gravity, and gravity bends light. Massive galaxy clusters act as giant warped lenses, stretching the images of more distant galaxies into arcs. By measuring that distortion, astronomers can map where the mass actually is.\n\nThe Bullet Cluster is two galaxy clusters that collided. In 2006 researchers showed that most of the ordinary matter, hot gas, had been slowed and piled up in the middle. Most of the mass, revealed by lensing, had passed straight through. This was widely seen as strong evidence that dark matter is a real substance, separate from ordinary matter."],
    ["What could it be?", "Dark matter is probably an unknown kind of particle that barely interacts with anything, and none has yet been detected.",
      "Dark matter is not simply dim stars, dust or black holes. Observations rule out ordinary matter making up more than a small fraction of it. Most physicists think it is a particle outside the standard model of particle physics, one that interacts with almost nothing except through gravity. Candidates include weakly interacting massive particles, nicknamed WIMPs, and extremely light particles called axions.\n\nExperiments deep underground, shielded from cosmic rays, wait for a dark matter particle to bump into an atom. Australia's Stawell Underground Physics Laboratory, in a Victorian gold mine, hosts one. So far there is no confirmed detection, and a minority of scientists suspect gravity itself needs modifying."],
    ["A speeding-up universe", "In 1998 two teams found that distant supernovae were fainter than expected, so the universe's expansion is accelerating.",
      "Astronomers have known since the 1920s that the universe is expanding. The natural assumption was that gravity would slow it. In the 1990s two rival teams set out to measure the slowdown using Type Ia supernovae, exploding white dwarfs with a predictable peak brightness that work as cosmic distance markers.\n\nSaul Perlmutter led one team in the United States. Brian Schmidt led the other, the High-Z Supernova Search Team, at the Australian National University's Mount Stromlo Observatory, with Adam Riess making key analyses. In 1998 both found the same startling result: distant supernovae were fainter than expected, so the expansion is accelerating. The three shared the 2011 Nobel Prize in Physics."],
    ["Dark energy", "Dark energy is the name for whatever drives the acceleration, and it makes up about 68 percent of the universe.",
      "Something seems to be pushing space apart. Physicists call it dark energy, a name for the effect rather than an explanation. The leading idea is that empty space carries a small, constant energy, like the cosmological constant Einstein added to his equations and later abandoned. Yet quantum theory predicts a value wildly larger than observed, one of science's biggest mismatches.\n\nThe universe's budget appears to be about 68 percent dark energy, 27 percent dark matter and only 5 percent ordinary matter. Recent results from the Dark Energy Spectroscopic Instrument in Arizona hint that dark energy may be weakening, but they are not yet conclusive. We can measure the universe precisely and still not say what most of it is."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did Vera Rubin find in spiral galaxies?", ["Outer stars orbit nearly as fast as inner ones, implying extra unseen mass","Outer stars orbit much slower, as planets do in a solar system","Stars at the edge are moving away from the galaxy centre","Galaxies spin faster the fewer stars they contain"], 0, "Flat orbital speeds far from the centre point to an invisible halo of extra mass."],
    [1, "What did the Bullet Cluster collision suggest about dark matter?", ["Most of the mass passed through, separate from the slowed gas","It is made of the hot gas left in the middle","It was destroyed when the clusters collided","It is just dim stars hidden behind the clusters"], 0, "Mass revealed by lensing separated from the gas, so dark matter behaves like a real substance."],
    [2, "What do most physicists think dark matter is?", ["Dim stars, dust and black holes we have not yet counted","A particle outside the standard model that barely interacts","A form of light too faint for telescopes to see","Ordinary matter that is simply very cold gas"], 1, "Ordinary objects are ruled out as the main part, so an unknown particle is the leading idea."],
    [3, "Why did faint distant supernovae surprise astronomers in 1998?", ["They showed the universe's expansion is speeding up","They proved the expansion had stopped altogether","They showed supernovae are not reliable distance markers","They showed gravity was slowing expansion faster than expected"], 0, "Supernovae fainter than expected meant they were further away, so expansion has accelerated."],
    [4, "Roughly how much of the universe is ordinary matter?", ["About 68 percent","About 27 percent","About 5 percent","About 50 percent"], 2, "Stars, planets and people are only about 5 percent, with dark matter 27 and dark energy 68."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Everything we can see is only about 5 percent of the universe. What is the rest?"});

